1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,750
Start in Department Settings.

2
00:00:02,170 --> 00:00:05,969
Open Calls & Dispatch and
choose Call & Dispatch Settings.

3
00:00:06,489 --> 00:00:10,069
This lesson follows the complete
setup of a Personnel check-in rule,

4
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:12,579
then tests it on one fictional call.

5
00:00:13,069 --> 00:00:17,120
Check-ins help coordinators organize
agreed reporting updates, including

6
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:20,360
community care visits and mental
health response assignments.

7
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:24,510
Department administrators configure
the rules; operators use the

8
00:00:24,510 --> 00:00:26,270
resulting timers and history.

9
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,329
Find Check-In Timer Settings.

10
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:32,280
Auto-Enable Check-In Timers for
New Calls controls whether new

11
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:34,139
calls start with timers enabled.

12
00:00:34,549 --> 00:00:36,500
It is off in this training department.

13
00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:40,490
We leave it off and explicitly
enable one practice call later.

14
00:00:40,799 --> 00:00:43,719
This separates department-wide
enablement from the reporting

15
00:00:43,719 --> 00:00:47,979
interval and from the decision to
use timers on an individual incident.

16
00:00:48,109 --> 00:00:52,440
Under Add New Default Timer, choose
Personnel as the Target Type.

17
00:00:52,909 --> 00:00:56,930
Set Duration to thirty minutes and
Warning Threshold to five minutes.

18
00:00:57,290 --> 00:00:58,819
Leave Enabled selected.

19
00:00:59,269 --> 00:01:04,450
Active States can restrict when a timer
applies; leaving it empty uses All States.

20
00:01:04,819 --> 00:01:08,000
Review the values, then
select Add Timer Config.

21
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:12,409
Use intervals and state filters that
your department has agreed for its work.

22
00:01:12,589 --> 00:01:17,170
Verify the saved Personnel row in
Default Timer Configurations: thirty

23
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:22,419
minutes, a five-minute warning threshold,
Enabled Yes, and Active States All.

24
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:26,780
This default applies when an enabled
timer has no matching override.

25
00:01:27,179 --> 00:01:30,789
In the current timer logic, the
warning period follows the duration:

26
00:01:31,139 --> 00:01:34,350
a thirty-minute interval enters
Warning at thirty minutes and

27
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:36,070
becomes overdue at thirty-five.

28
00:01:36,590 --> 00:01:39,619
The configured threshold is the
length of that warning period.

29
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:44,160
Use Add New Override for a rule
tied to a call type or priority.

30
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:49,980
Here Call Type ID is Rescue, Call
Priority is Any, Target Type is

31
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:54,629
Personnel, Duration is forty-five
minutes, and Warning Threshold is ten.

32
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:58,569
We clear Enabled to save this
example in a disabled state.

33
00:01:58,969 --> 00:02:00,469
Active States is empty.

34
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:04,409
Select Add Override after
reviewing the scope and values.

35
00:02:04,619 --> 00:02:07,379
Review Call Type / Priority Overrides.

36
00:02:07,810 --> 00:02:12,049
The Rescue row now shows forty-five
minutes, ten warning minutes, and

37
00:02:12,099 --> 00:02:14,980
Enabled No, so that example is inactive.

38
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:19,379
The existing Investigation Personnel
rule is enabled for sixty minutes

39
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:21,180
with a five-minute warning threshold.

40
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:24,879
Matching enabled overrides take
precedence over the default.

41
00:02:25,199 --> 00:02:28,719
We will use Investigation to
test that sixty-minute rule while

42
00:02:28,719 --> 00:02:30,650
retaining the thirty-minute default.

43
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,720
On New Call, enter a clear training Name
and choose the intended Priority and Type.

44
00:02:36,189 --> 00:02:39,480
Our type is Investigation
and the priority is Routine.

45
00:02:39,999 --> 00:02:43,989
The Call Identifier gives this exercise
another recognizable reference.

46
00:02:44,329 --> 00:02:48,320
Keep the scenario fictional and
identify it clearly so other operators

47
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,129
can distinguish it from normal work.

48
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,980
We use a supplied fictional
location for this software exercise.

49
00:02:54,189 --> 00:02:57,660
In Personnel, select Wallace
Boden for this practice call.

50
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:02,399
Review the selected row rather than
assuming a station, role, or group

51
00:03:02,399 --> 00:03:04,160
contains the intended recipient.

52
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:08,370
For a real rollout, test with an
agreed training audience first.

53
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:12,910
A call may have several participants,
so the recipient scope matters when you

54
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:15,059
review which personnel timers appear.

55
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:18,910
Near the bottom of the form,
select Enable Check-In Timers.

56
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:23,479
This is the per-call choice we planned
while automatic enablement remained off.

57
00:03:23,849 --> 00:03:27,109
Review the rest of the call
and its recipients, then choose

58
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:28,790
Create and Dispatch Call.

59
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:32,740
Saving timer configuration alone
does not opt this call into

60
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:36,859
timers; this checkbox makes that
choice explicit for the exercise.

61
00:03:37,079 --> 00:03:39,930
Open the new call and
inspect Check-In Timers.

62
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:45,100
Wallace Boden has a Personnel timer,
the state is OK, and Time Remaining

63
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:46,890
is just under sixty minutes.

64
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:50,720
This call uses the enabled
Investigation override rather

65
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:52,180
than the thirty-minute default.

66
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:56,540
Open Show Check-In History to
inspect the saved entries; this

67
00:03:56,540 --> 00:03:58,570
new exercise initially has none.

68
00:03:59,089 --> 00:04:02,949
This readback checks the effect of
the configuration on an actual call.

69
00:04:03,190 --> 00:04:05,459
The Check In dialog provides a Note field.

70
00:04:05,989 --> 00:04:08,989
Enter a concise update under
your department procedure,

71
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:11,300
review it, and select Check In.

72
00:04:11,949 --> 00:04:15,899
Our note identifies a software practice
check-in for this timer lesson.

73
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:19,820
It makes no claim about physical
arrival or device location.

74
00:04:20,269 --> 00:04:23,419
The note belongs to the check-in
record, so it should explain

75
00:04:23,419 --> 00:04:26,710
the update clearly enough for
another coordinator to understand.

76
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:30,060
Review the timer and saved
history after confirmation.

77
00:04:30,439 --> 00:04:34,390
The table shows Timestamp,
Who, Type, and Note.

78
00:04:34,809 --> 00:04:38,619
Our new Personnel entry contains
the exact practice note we entered.

79
00:04:38,970 --> 00:04:41,039
The timer has begun a new interval.

80
00:04:41,369 --> 00:04:44,589
Checking the saved history
distinguishes a completed update

81
00:04:44,589 --> 00:04:46,230
from text left in a dialog.

82
00:04:46,650 --> 00:04:51,679
Keep status reporting, check-in reporting,
and any physical safety verification under

83
00:04:51,679 --> 00:04:53,750
their appropriate department procedures.

84
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:56,080
Finish the exercise through Close Call.

85
00:04:56,560 --> 00:05:01,410
Review Completed Type, Send
Notification, and Completed Notes.

86
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:06,489
We use Closed, leave Send Notification
off for this local practice, and

87
00:05:06,489 --> 00:05:08,310
record a clear completion note.

88
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:10,389
Then select Close Call.

89
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:14,780
Review these choices for your own
audience: the notification option

90
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:18,169
changes whether the closing event
is sent to the call participants.

91
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:22,609
Verify that the call now shows Closed
and the expected Completed Notes.

92
00:05:23,019 --> 00:05:26,070
Its saved check-in history
remains available for review.

93
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:30,719
The same configuration and verification
habits apply to search and rescue,

94
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:36,350
security companies, wildland contractors,
and industrial response teams: choose

95
00:05:36,350 --> 00:05:40,139
the reporting target, agree the
interval, test the matching rule,

96
00:05:40,399 --> 00:05:44,299
and check the resulting record before
extending the setup to everyday work.

